llvm-3.3 backport patch: the strong rules of the protected frame's layout (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION for every function that carries a stack
canary, and only for those, on top of llvm-3.3-ssplayout-01: after the guard
the prolog and epilog inserter and the local stack slot pass place the large
arrays, then the small arrays, then the objects whose address is taken, each
group in frame-index order, and then every other object in index order. When
stack coloring merges one slot into another it moves the merged alloca's
classification to the slot that stays (an object whose address is taken does
not overwrite an array, a small array does not overwrite a large one). It
ports two upstream commits, both after the 3.3 branch:

  r200601  24c7f063333b  2014-02-01  Josh Magee, "[stackprotector] Implement
           the sspstrong rules for stack layout."
  r199684  a69e5b8b9d32  2014-01-20  Hal Finkel, "Update StackProtector when
           coloring merges stack slots"

Adapted to 3.3: r199684 sits in the loop of StackColoring::remapInstructions
that 3.3 already has over the remapped slots; its parent r199658
(cd9569c19e, the same day, "Update IR when merging slots in stack
coloring", which rewrites the IR uses of the merged alloca for alias
analysis) is a separate change and is not ported. Later upstream changes to
the pass after the bracket (r206486, adfde5fef6f6 of 2014-04-17, "[stack
protector] Make the StackProtector pass respect ssp-buffer-size"), are
outside D32's bracket and are not ported. docs/DECISIONS.md D35.

